winewayland: put the primary monitor at (0,0) (Premiere clicks landed above the pointer on GNOME)

Premiere 2026 on a two-monitor GNOME desktop could not be clicked in the bottom of its window: clicks
landed above the pointer (GitHub Neutron#16). The reporter's 4K monitor at 125% sits 443 logical px
lower than the 1080p monitor beside it, so no output is at logical (0,0). The arrangement then made
the first output primary at (0,443). Windows always has the primary monitor at (0,0), and Premiere
relies on it: it computes its maximized position from work-area coordinates as if they were relative
to the primary, so it maximized 443 px further down, past the bottom of the virtual screen (reporter:
window (-4,922)-(3762,3042) on a monitor ending at y=2603). Every pointer position in the lower part
of the window mapped below the screen and was clamped to its edge (23527 of 35280 motion events in the
reporter's trace).

After overlap resolution, the whole layout is now translated so the primary output sits at the
origin; other outputs may be at negative coordinates, as on Windows. Layouts that already have an
output at (0,0), including every single-monitor setup, are unchanged. Kill switch:
NEUTRON_PRIMARY_ORIGIN=0.

Harness: preserved-fixes/harnesses/monitors.c prints the monitor layout. Nested mutter 51 with the
reporter's arrangement (devkit monitor at 125%, primary, logical (0,443); a 1920x1080 at 100% at
(1792,0)): before, the primary is at (0,354) and no monitor covers (0,0); patched, the primary is at
(0,0) and the other at (2240,-284). Premiere 2026 there with fresh preferences: before, maximized at
(0,882)-(2240,2282) with the monitor ending at y=1843, and the window's bottom edge mapped to y=2281;
patched, maximized at exactly the monitor, (0,0)-(2240,1400), and 821 pointer events down to the
bottom edge all mapped inside the screen (lowest y=1399).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/display.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/display.c
@@ -183,6 +183,32 @@
      * to right, but ensure the primary output is first. */
     qsort(output_info_array->data, num_outputs, sizeof(struct output_info),
           output_info_cmp_primary_x_y);
+
+    /* NEUTRON (GitHub Neutron#16): Windows always has the primary monitor at (0,0), and apps rely on
+     * it. With no output at logical (0,0) -- a 125% 4K monitor placed 443 px lower than the 1080p one
+     * beside it -- the first output became primary at (0,443). Premiere computes its maximized
+     * position from work-area coordinates as if they were relative to the primary, so it maximized
+     * a further 443 px down, past the bottom of the virtual screen; every pointer position in the
+     * lower part of the window was clamped to the screen edge and clicks there landed above the
+     * pointer. Translate the whole layout so the primary sits at the origin (others may then be at
+     * negative coordinates, as on Windows). Layouts with an output at (0,0) are unchanged.
+     * Kill switch: NEUTRON_PRIMARY_ORIGIN=0. */
+    if (num_outputs)
+    {
+        static int on = -1;
+        struct output_info *primary = output_info_array->data;
+        int dx = primary->x, dy = primary->y;
+
+        if (on < 0) on = neutron_gate("NEUTRON_PRIMARY_ORIGIN", TRUE);
+        if (on && (dx || dy))
+        {
+            wl_array_for_each(info, output_info_array)
+            {
+                info->x -= dx;
+                info->y -= dy;
+            }
+        }
+    }
 }
 
 /* NEUTRON: (re)compute each output's arranged physical position into output->current.x/y, running the
